            <ArticleTitle>Altered allergen binding capacities of Amb a 1-specific IgE and IgG4 from ragweed-sensitive patients receiving immunotherapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The mechanisms for the effectiveness of allergen immunotherapy (IT) are not well understood. The binding potential for immunoglobulins is a function of both antibody concentration and affinity (K(A)).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose was to perform a cross-sectional preliminary study to investigate any differences in allergen-specific antibody affinity and concentration following ragweed immunotherapy by introducing a new concept of antibody binding capacity ([Ig] X K(A)).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The binding capacity of allergen-specific IgE and IgG4 was determined for ragweed-allergic individuals undergoing ragweed immunotherapy and compared with the capacity of ragweed-specific IgE and IgG4 for allergic individuals not receiving immunotherapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean binding capacity for IgG4 after long-term immunotherapy was 1.6 log units higher (P &lt; .0001) than for individuals not receiving IT. The binding capacity for allergen-specific IgE was 1.2 log units lower following long-term immunotherapy (P &lt; .0001) compared with individuals not receiving ragweed IT.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We hypothesize that a primary effect of immunotherapy is to increase IgG4 binding capacity and concomitantly decrease IgE binding capacity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
